---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---
<BaseLayout title="Particle Topology" description="Hydrodynamic intersection topology: Depth as Mass, Tilt as Charge.">
  <article class="container" style="padding-top: 4rem; padding-bottom: 4rem; max-width: 800px; margin: 0 auto;">
    <h1>Hydrodynamic Particle Topology</h1>
    <p class="lead">The Standard Model particle zoo is an illusion of perspective. What we perceive as a specific particle is simply the result of the specific geometric angle and depth at which our sweeping timeline intersects a static TSC entity.</p>

    <figure style="margin: 2rem 0; text-align: center;">
      <div style="background: #f1f5f9; border-radius: 12px; display: flex; align-items: center; justify-content: center; color: #64748b; border: 2px dashed #cbd5e1;">
        <img src="/images/Hydrodynamic_Particle_Map_Framework.png" alt="Simplified Particle Map" style="width: 100%; height: auto; border-radius: 8px;" />
      </div>
      <figcaption style="color: #64748b; font-size: 0.9rem; margin-top: 0.5rem; font-style: italic;">Fig 2. Dual-dial mapping: Intersection Depth (Mass) and Tilt (Charge).</figcaption>
    </figure>

    <h2>The River and the Stick Analogy</h2>
    <p>Visualize the timeline as a flowing river. A TSC flow line acts like a straight stick dropped into this moving water. Particle properties are controlled by two independent geometric dials:</p>

    <h3>Dial 1: Intersection Depth (Rest Mass)</h3>
    <p>Mass is a measure of hydrodynamic resistance against the timeline sweep.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Grazing Depth (0°):</strong> Flat on water. Zero resistance → <strong>Photons</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Shallow Depth (80°):</strong> Barely submerged. Minimal drag → <strong>Electrons</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Steep Depth (0° Perpendicular):</strong> Jammed into the riverbed. Maximum resistance → <strong>Protons</strong>.</li>
    </ul>

    <h3>Dial 2: Intersection Tilt (Electric Charge)</h3>
    <p>Charge is a measure of asymmetric fluid vortices.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Perfect Symmetry (45°):</strong> Water hits left and right equally. Vortices cancel → <strong>Neutral (Neutrons)</strong>.</li>
      <li><strong>Asymmetric Tilt:</strong> Water strikes one side harder. A dominant vortex forms. Push = Positive. Pull = Negative.</li>
    </ul>

    <div style="margin-top: 2rem; padding: 1rem; background: #eff6ff; border-left: 4px solid #3b82f6; border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0;">
      <h3 style="margin-top: 0; color: #1e40af;">The 45-Degree Lock</h3>
      <p style="margin-bottom: 0; color: #1e3a8a;">The 45-degree symmetric intersection is a geometric stability lock. Any deviation from this angle triggers a topological change that converts the particle from invisible background mass into active, interacting matter.</p>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>